> [!WARNING]
> **DeepSkin is not a medical device.** It is an educational project and must not be used as a substitute for professional diagnosis. If you are worried about a skin lesion, see a dermatologist.

## Overview

DeepSkin is an end-to-end deep learning pipeline that classifies dermatoscopic images into **seven types of skin lesion**. It is trained on **HAM10000**, a collection of 10,015 dermatoscopic images, and aims to support rapid preliminary screening that helps flag skin cancer early.

Three things make up the project:

- **A fair benchmark.** A custom CNN baseline is compared against three ImageNet-pretrained architectures (MobileNetV2, DenseNet121, EfficientNet) under the same preprocessing.
- **A selection step.** The final model is chosen by balancing accuracy, inference speed, and model size, rather than accuracy alone.
- **A working demo.** A Streamlit app lets you upload an image and see per-class confidence scores immediately.

<br>

## Results

All models were evaluated on the same HAM10000 test split.

<div align="center">
  <img src="assets/results.svg" alt="Animated bar chart of test accuracy by model" width="100%">
</div>


| Model | Architecture idea | Accuracy | Takeaway |
|:--|:--|:--:|:--|
| Custom CNN | 4 conv blocks + dense head | ~68% | Baseline |
| MobileNetV2 | Depthwise separable convolutions | ~73% | Fastest inference |
| EfficientNet | Compound scaling | ~74% | Best efficiency/accuracy trade-off |
| **DenseNet121** | Dense connections | **>75%** | **Selected: highest accuracy** |

DenseNet121 is the selected model. It scored the highest accuracy on the 7-class task while keeping a good balance of model size and inference speed.

