## The 7 lesion classes

| Code | Lesion | Category |
|:--:|:--|:--|
| `akiec` | Actinic keratoses | 🟡 Pre-cancerous |
| `bcc` | Basal cell carcinoma | 🔴 Malignant |
| `bkl` | Benign keratosis | 🟢 Benign |
| `df` | Dermatofibroma | 🟢 Benign |
| `mel` | Melanoma | 🔴 Malignant |
| `nv` | Melanocytic nevi | 🟢 Benign |
| `vasc` | Vascular lesions | 🟢 Benign |

> [!NOTE]
> **The dataset is heavily imbalanced.** Melanocytic nevi (`nv`) make up about 67% of all samples. Stratified splits keep the class distribution the same across train, validation, and test, and class weights are used during training to compensate.

<br>

## Pipeline

<div align="center">
  <img src="assets/inference.svg" alt="Animated diagram: dermatoscopic image through DenseNet121 to seven class confidence bars" width="100%">
</div>

<sub>The class confidences shown in the animation are illustrative, not real model output.</sub>

<details>
<summary><b>1. Data preprocessing</b></summary>

<br>

- 10,015 dermatoscopic images, resized to each model's input size
- Stratified splits preserve class balance
- Augmentation: rotation, horizontal and vertical flips, zoom, brightness, shear

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>2. Model training</b></summary>

<br>

- Custom CNN baseline (4 conv blocks + dense classifier)
- Transfer learning from ImageNet weights for MobileNetV2, DenseNet121, and EfficientNet
- Class weights to handle the imbalance
- Early stopping and learning rate scheduling

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>3. Evaluation and selection</b></summary>

<br>

- Per-class precision, recall, F1, and ROC-AUC
- Confusion matrix analysis
- Automated model selection that weighs accuracy, speed, and model size
- Error analysis on the worst-performing classes

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>4. Deployment</b></summary>

<br>

- Streamlit web app with real-time image upload
- Confidence score for every class
- Class-wise metrics dashboard

</details>

<br>

## Quick start

```bash
# 1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/sonubiswal/deepskin-skin-cancer-detection.git
cd deepskin-skin-cancer-detection

# 2. Install dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 3. Train a model (pick one)
python train_densenet.py
python train_cnn.py
python train_efficientnet.py

# 4. Launch the web demo
streamlit run app.py
```

<br>

## Citation

DeepSkin is trained on the HAM10000 dataset. If you use the data, please cite the original paper:

> Tschandl, P., Rosendahl, C. & Kittler, H. *The HAM10000 dataset, a large collection of multi-source dermatoscopic images of common pigmented skin lesions.* Scientific Data 5, 180161 (2018). [doi:10.1038/sdata.2018.161](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2018.161)

